# Path, Trajectory, Affordance, And Action-Manifold Updates

Related planning note: [thoughts.md](../thoughts.md)

This is the canonical design document for the next generation of CITV path,
trajectory, affordance, and animation-planning work. It captures the discussion
about where the current path system is strong, where it is under-specified, and
how to evolve it into an open-vocabulary, depth-aware, scene-aware action
manifold system.

The central point is blunt: better shortest-path search is not enough. The
project needs richer scene representation and richer motion contracts. A path
for animation is not always a line. It can be a ribbon, blob, volume, contour,
portal, contact patch, occlusion pulse, or local effect field depending on the
action and the scene.

## 1. Purpose And Scope

This document is the implementation source of truth for future path and
trajectory upgrades. It is intentionally design-first. It should be reviewed
before code changes begin.

The immediate goal is to improve scene-aware animation contracts, not merely to
find shorter or smoother paths. A route should carry enough evidence for the
animation system to decide:

- where motion can happen;
- what kind of motion is plausible;
- how the actor or effect should scale with depth;
- whether it is visible, partially occluded, hidden, reflected, or fading;
- what scene, object, mask, and relation evidence supports or rejects it.

The document covers:

- current codebase behavior;
- current pitfalls;
- the proposed conceptual model;
- scene-level, object-level, and mask-level affordance understanding;
- open-vocabulary action grounding;
- dense/global/region/object/mask caption evidence;
- depth, geometry, and pixel grounding;
- occlusion and rendering awareness;
- output contracts;
- formulas;
- computational costs;
- implementation roadmap;
- acceptance criteria.

## 2. Current Codebase State

CITV currently builds a scene graph from one or more RGB images. The
package-native staged pipeline runs roughly as:

```text
image
 -> preprocessing / optional undistortion
 -> camera intrinsics
 -> metric depth
 -> depth regions
 -> GroundingDINO + SAM2 segmentation
 -> per-mask labelling and depth geometry
 -> relation prediction
 -> visualization
 -> path export
 -> animation export
 -> captions
 -> scene JSON
```

The staged runner lives in `scene_understanding/stages/full_run.py`. It passes a
`PipelineContext` through each stage. That context carries image data,
intrinsics, metric depth, detections, relations, region labels, region metadata,
objects, mask hierarchy, path exports, and extra stage data.

### Current strengths

- Metric depth is first-class. `depth.py` uses CLIP to choose indoor/outdoor
  Depth Anything V2 Metric and returns depth in metres.
- Objects carry useful depth geometry: mask centroid, depth stats,
  depth-weighted 3D coordinates, raw/eroded variants, region assignment, and
  plausibility notes.
- The pipeline has strong segmentation and labelling inputs: GroundingDINO,
  SAM2, Florence-2, RAM++ tags/captions, and Pix2SG-style relation scaffolding.
- The legacy/root pipeline also contains richer caption-oriented behavior:
  dense or bundled descriptions of scenes, regions, objects, masks, path
  explanations, and uncertainty notes. Future implementation should preserve
  the useful caption evidence in package-native staged form instead of treating
  captions as final prose only.
- The pathing package already contains good primitives: traversability speed
  maps, walkable masks, semantic cost layers, FMM path planning, navigation
  zones, trajectory bundles, animation components, and QA rendering.
- FMM is a reasonable planner for continuous cost fields. The planner is not the
  weakest part of the system.

### Current weaknesses

- Staged path export is simpler than the richer root monolith. The staged path
  stage mostly builds an obstacle mask, traversability speed map, walkable
  snapshot, and FMM object-to-object paths. It does not yet expose all of the
  richer semantic cost, navigation-zone, provenance, path atlas, path
  diagnostics, and motion-contract signals available elsewhere in the repo.
- Regions are primarily depth partitions. A depth region can contain floor,
  wall, sky, road, table surface, grass, water, or clutter. Those have very
  different motion implications.
- Objects are often treated too bluntly. Object masks become obstacles too
  easily, even though a rug, road, water surface, doorway, table, mirror, glass
  pane, chair, person, and sky region need different behavior.
- Object-to-object paths frequently start/end at mask centroids. For animation,
  actors usually need approach/contact anchors, foot anchors, occlusion
  boundaries, entry/exit points, or support patches instead of centroids.
- Staged paths are mostly `polyline_2d` plus confidence. They need
  `polyline_3d`, depth traces, semantic traces, support traces, visibility
  traces, width profiles, and action/manifold types.
- Trajectories are under-informed when path hypotheses do not include rich 3D
  and semantic evidence. The animation stage can infer walk/run/careful/climb
  more reliably only if paths carry richer signals.
- Caption evidence is not yet a first-class staged path/action input. Captions
  currently help labelling and documentation, but path generation should also
  consume scene captions, dense region captions, object captions, mask captions,
  path descriptions, and caption uncertainty as structured semantic evidence.

### Root monolith / staged parity gap

The root `scene_understanding.py` contains richer path export logic than the
package-native staged `paths_export.py`. It includes concepts such as semantic
cost maps, soft obstacles, pair proposals, hybrid scoring, path descriptions,
diagnostics, path atlases, motion contracts, animation components, and QA
rendering. It also has richer captioning/documentation-style outputs than the
current staged path/action contract. The staged path exporter is more compact
and easier to reason about, but it has not reached parity with that richer
behavior.

The next implementation should not blindly copy the monolith. It should promote
the useful concepts into clean staged modules and keep the output contracts
additive and testable.

## 3. Core Pitfalls

### Pitfall 1: better pathfinding does not equal better animation

FMM can find a good geodesic on a cost field. If the cost field does not know
floor from sky, wall from doorway, water from rug, or mirror from passable
portal, the path can still be visually wrong.

The strongest improvement is a better scene/action representation feeding the
planner, not a new shortest-path algorithm.

### Pitfall 2: not every action is a polyline

Walking can be a line or ribbon. But holding, peeking, hiding, disappearing,
wobbling, reflecting, flying, swimming, orbiting, entering, emerging, and
floating are not naturally represented as a single centerline.

A path system for animation must support multiple manifold types.

### Pitfall 3: do not hard-code label behavior

Avoid logic like:

```text
if label == "water": swimmable = true
if label == "chair": obstacle = true
if label == "sky": flyable = true
```

That does not scale. It fails on synonyms, unusual labels, captions, and
ambiguous objects.

Use open-vocabulary semantic evidence:

```text
swimmable_score =
  similarity(entity_caption, "liquid, water, pool, river, ocean, swimmable surface")
  fused with depth, geometry, mask shape, and relations
```

### Pitfall 4: do not ignore occlusion and depth ordering

Animation is rendered into an image. A route that crosses behind a chair should
not draw the actor fully in front of the chair. A disappearing action should not
only move to a point; it should fade, shrink, or become hidden according to the
target mask and depth ordering.

Every useful trajectory should be able to report visibility and render layer.

### Pitfall 5: do not add heavy models before wiring existing signals

The repo already has many useful local signals. Before adding more models, first
wire existing staged outputs into richer path/action contracts:

- metric depth;
- 3D coordinates;
- masks;
- labels;
- captions;
- RAM++ tags;
- region metadata;
- relations;
- semantic cost modules;
- navigation zones;
- trajectory bundles.

Heavy geometry/VLM providers can be optional later.

## 4. New Conceptual Model

The new flow should be:

```text
scene graph
 -> dense caption evidence
 -> scene/object/mask affordance understanding
 -> open-vocabulary action grounding
 -> action-manifold generation
 -> trajectory/render contract generation
 -> QA overlays and animation exports
```

This replaces "path only" thinking with action-aware motion. A normal walking
path remains a valid action manifold, but it is only one type.

### Action manifold types

`centerline_path`

- A thin centerline through valid support space.
- Best for simple walk/run/drive routes.

`ribbon_path`

- A centerline with width, boundaries, depth-aware scale, and clearance.
- Best for body-aware walking/running/crawling.

`blob_path`

- An area-based path or motion zone.
- Best for swimming, floating, hovering, idle movement, liquid movement, and
  soft effects.

`volume_path`

- A projected 2D/3D aerial volume.
- Best for flying, floating through sky/open air, smoke-like movement, or
  background aerial motion.

`contour_path`

- A route around or along an object boundary.
- Best for orbiting, inspecting, circling, peeking along an edge, or moving
  around obstacles.

`interior_path`

- A route inside a mask or region.
- Best for moving within rugs, roads, water surfaces, floor patches, containers,
  or large support masks.

`portal_path`

- A route into or out of a doorway, mirror, shadow, opening, tunnel, gate,
  window, or water surface.
- Best for entering, exiting, appearing, disappearing, or emerging.

`occlusion_pulse`

- A short local motion near an occlusion boundary.
- Best for peeking, hiding, revealing, ducking behind an object, or partial
  emergence.

`contact_patch`

- A local constraint between actor and object.
- Best for holding, touching, grabbing, dragging, pushing, leaning, sitting, or
  placing.

`effect_field`

- A local animation/effect field rather than physical traversal.
- Best for wobble, shimmer, reflection, dissolve, ripple, glass/mirror effects,
  or magical/non-physical scene actions.

## 5. Scene-Level Understanding

Scene-level understanding should answer:

```text
What parts of the image support motion?
What parts block motion?
What parts allow aerial, liquid, reflective, portal, or effect actions?
What global topology connects those areas?
```

### Scene-level entities

Support surfaces:

- floor;
- ground;
- sidewalk;
- road;
- path;
- rug;
- tabletop;
- platform;
- bridge;
- stair tread;
- ramp;
- water surface for floating actors.

Blocking surfaces:

- wall;
- large furniture body;
- vehicle body;
- tree trunk;
- fence;
- closed door;
- cabinet;
- foreground person or animal, depending on actor/action.

Open-air / sky regions:

- sky;
- open space above ground;
- far background air;
- aerial corridor.

Liquid regions:

- water;
- pool;
- lake;
- river;
- sea;
- puddle;
- fluid/liquid container surface.

Reflective / transparent regions:

- mirror;
- glass;
- window;
- glossy surface;
- transparent pane;
- reflective water.

Portals and transitions:

- doorway;
- hallway opening;
- gate;
- arch;
- tunnel;
- window;
- mirror-like portal;
- shadow/occluder entry;
- water entry/exit edge.

Cluttered / uncertain areas:

- high depth noise;
- dense object overlap;
- ambiguous mask hierarchy;
- far background;
- textureless surfaces;
- transparent objects with low depth separation.

### Why scene-level understanding matters

Without scene-level semantics, a path can cross a wall because the wall is just
part of a connected depth region. With support semantics, the system can prefer:

```text
floor -> doorway threshold -> hallway floor
```

instead of:

```text
foreground object centroid -> arbitrary background region -> target centroid
```

Scene-level understanding also lets actor type change the valid route:

- ground actor uses floor/road/sidewalk;
- flying actor can use sky/open-air volume;
- swimming actor can use water/liquid masks;
- small object can move on tabletop;
- reflection/effect action can use mirror/glass without treating it as a normal
  walkable surface.

## 6. Object-Level Understanding

Object-level understanding should answer:

```text
What role does this object play in motion or action?
Can it be approached, held, hidden behind, entered, climbed, crossed, reflected in,
avoided, or used as a support?
Where are the useful anchors?
```

### Object roles

`actor`

- An entity that can initiate motion or action.
- Examples: person, animal, vehicle, animated object, generated character.

`hard_obstacle`

- Usually blocks traversal for the current actor.
- Example: wall, table body, closed cabinet, vehicle body.

`soft_obstacle`

- Can be avoided but may be crossed, stepped over, climbed, jumped, or treated as
  high cost depending on actor and action.
- Example: stool, curb, small box, low object.

`support`

- Can hold or support motion/contact.
- Example: floor, road, table surface, chair seat, platform.

`container`

- May allow inside/enter/exit/contain actions.
- Example: box, bowl, cup, doorway-like container, room-like area.

`portal`

- May allow through/enter/exit/disappear/emerge actions.
- Example: doorway, gate, window, tunnel, mirror if action is effect-like.

`liquid`

- Supports swim, float, splash, ripple, sink, or liquid surface actions.

`reflective_transparent`

- Supports reflect, shimmer, wobble, fade, pass-through only if action and
  geometry make sense.

`interaction_target`

- Can be held, touched, approached, looked at, opened, sat on, pushed, pulled,
  or otherwise acted on.

`occluder`

- Can hide or partially hide actors/effects.
- Useful for peeking, hiding, emerging, and disappearing behind objects.

### Object anchors

Approach points:

- Points near the object where an actor can plausibly stand, arrive, or interact.
- Should be outside hard obstacle masks and on valid support.

Contact points:

- Points on the object boundary or support/contact patch.
- Useful for hold, touch, push, pull, sit, lean, grab.

Occlusion boundary points:

- Boundary segments where an actor can hide, peek, emerge, or disappear.
- Derived from object contour, depth edge, and foreground/background ordering.

Support/contact patches:

- Subregions of the mask that can support contact or on-object motion.
- Example: chair seat, tabletop, road surface, water surface.

Entry/exit points:

- Points on portal/liquid/container masks where enter/exit makes sense.
- Example: doorway threshold, water edge, opening boundary, mirror face.

